window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["autogen"]=[{"id":"autogen-f0001","card":"q-autogen-001-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"What is an AI agent, and what parts make it up?","a":"<p>An agent is an LLM wrapped with tools, memory and context so it can act toward a goal instead of only producing text. Typical parts: the LLM for language understanding, tools (APIs, calculators, code execution), memory (a database or conversation history), and access to documents or other data. The LLM decides; the tools do the acting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0002","card":"q-autogen-001-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"What is AutoGen, and what can one AutoGen agent do?","a":"<p>AutoGen is Microsoft's open-source framework for multi-agent conversations. Each agent can send messages, receive messages and produce replies. The reply can come from an LLM, a code executor, a human, or a mix of these. Agents with different roles and tools solve a task by talking to each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0003","card":"q-autogen-001-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"Why use several specialised agents instead of one big LLM prompt?","a":"<p>Each agent has a short, focused prompt and tool set, so it does one job better. Multiple perspectives (for example writer plus reviewer) improve quality, you can add or swap agents without rewriting everything, and independent work can run in parallel. The trade-off is more LLM calls, so higher cost and latency, and more ways for the conversation to loop or drift.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0004","card":"q-autogen-001-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"When is a single agent the better choice than a multi-agent setup?","a":"<p>When the task is short, has one clear skill and needs no review step. Extra agents multiply token cost and latency, and add coordination failures such as loops and hand-off mistakes. Add agents only when roles, tools or checks truly differ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0005","card":"q-autogen-001-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"How would you break a complex problem down for a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>Split it into subtasks, give each subtask to an agent with one clear role and the tools it needs, let the agents exchange results, then combine the output. A typical chain is researcher gathers data, analyst draws insights, writer produces the report.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0006","card":"q-autogen-002-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, which agentic design patterns does AutoGen support, and what does each one give you?","a":"<p>Reflection: an agent's output is reviewed (by itself or a peer) and improved. Tool use: the LLM calls functions or APIs to calculate, search or query. Multi-agent collaboration: agents work together through sequential chats, nested chats or group chat. Human-in-the-loop and persistent memory (<code>Teachability</code>) are common additions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0007","card":"q-autogen-002-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"What conversation shapes can AutoGen agents have?","a":"<p>Two-way (one agent talks to one agent), multi-way (a group chat with several agents) and nested (an agent runs inner conversations with other agents before it replies). Collaboration can be sequential, parallel or hierarchical (manager and workers).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0008","card":"q-autogen-002-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"How does the reflection pattern improve an LLM answer, and what does it cost?","a":"<p>A second agent (or the same one in another role) critiques the draft, and the writer revises using that feedback. Quality usually rises because errors and gaps are caught. Cost and latency rise with every review round, so cap the number of rounds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0009","card":"q-autogen-002-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"How do AutoGen, LangChain, CrewAI, LangGraph and Semantic Kernel differ at a high level?","a":"<p>AutoGen focuses on conversations between agents. LangChain is a general toolkit for LLM applications. CrewAI is built around role-based agent crews. LangGraph models agent workflows as stateful graphs with explicit control flow. Semantic Kernel is Microsoft's SDK aimed at enterprise integration. Choose by how much control over flow you need: conversation-driven (AutoGen), graph-driven (LangGraph).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0010","card":"q-autogen-002-1-core-concepts-interview-essentials","sec":"autogen-s002","q":"Which AutoGen API generation do most tutorials with <code>import autogen</code> and <code>pyautogen</code> use, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>They use the AutoGen v0.2 API (<code>pyautogen</code>, classes such as <code>ConversableAgent</code>, <code>initiate_chat</code>, <code>GroupChat</code>). AutoGen v0.4 was a ground-up rewrite (<code>autogen-agentchat</code>, async, event-driven, team classes such as <code>RoundRobinGroupChat</code>), and many names and call styles differ. Check which version a codebase or answer refers to before copying code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0011","card":"q-autogen-003-2-setup-and-conversableagent","sec":"autogen-s003","q":"What is <code>ConversableAgent</code> in AutoGen v0.2, and which components does it combine?","a":"<p>It is the generic base agent class. It bundles four switchable parts: LLM configuration, a code executor, a function/tool executor and human-in-the-loop input. Roles differ only in which parts you turn on, so <code>AssistantAgent</code> and <code>UserProxyAgent</code> are specialisations of it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0012","card":"q-autogen-003-2-setup-and-conversableagent","sec":"autogen-s003","q":"How do you install AutoGen v0.2, and how do you import it?","a":"<p>Run <code>pip install pyautogen</code> and use <code>import autogen</code> or <code>from autogen import ConversableAgent</code>. The package name differs from the import name. In AutoGen v0.4 the packages are different (<code>autogen-agentchat</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0013","card":"q-autogen-003-2-setup-and-conversableagent","sec":"autogen-s003","q":"How would you make a role-specific agent, such as a researcher and a writer, with the same class?","a":"<p>Create two <code>ConversableAgent</code> instances with different <code>name</code> and <code>system_message</code> values and the same <code>llm_config</code>. The class and interface stay the same, and the system message defines the role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0014","card":"q-autogen-003-2-setup-and-conversableagent","sec":"autogen-s003","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why give agents descriptive names such as <code>marketing_assistant</code> instead of <code>agent1</code>?","a":"<p>The name appears in transcripts, logs and in group chat speaker selection, where the manager reads agent names. Clear names make logs readable and help the LLM choose the right speaker.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0015","card":"q-autogen-004-environment-variables-and-llm-config","sec":"autogen-s004","q":"Where should an API key live in an AutoGen project, and why?","a":"<p>In environment variables, usually a <code>.env</code> file loaded with <code>python-dotenv</code>, and the <code>.env</code> file must be in <code>.gitignore</code>. Keys hard-coded in source end up in version control and logs. In production use a secret manager or the platform's environment configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0016","card":"q-autogen-004-environment-variables-and-llm-config","sec":"autogen-s004","q":"What does <code>load_dotenv()</code> do and what does it return?","a":"<p>It reads key=value lines from a <code>.env</code> file into the process environment and returns <code>True</code> if a file was found and loaded, <code>False</code> otherwise. It does not raise when the file is missing, so check the return value if you depend on it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0017","card":"q-autogen-004-environment-variables-and-llm-config","sec":"autogen-s004","q":"Fill in the missing piece: how do you build an <code>llm_config</code> for OpenAI in AutoGen v0.2 without hard-coding the key?","a":"<p>Read the key from the environment: <code>\"api_key\": os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")</code>. The dict needs at least the model name and the key.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import os\n\nllm_config = {\n    &quot;model&quot;: &quot;gpt-4o&quot;,\n    &quot;api_key&quot;: ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"autogen-f0018","card":"q-autogen-004-environment-variables-and-llm-config","sec":"autogen-s004","q":"How would you configure AutoGen v0.2 to use a local model server, or to fall back across several models?","a":"<p>For a local server set <code>base_url</code> (an OpenAI-compatible endpoint) and <code>model</code> in the config. For several models, supply a list of config dicts (a <code>config_list</code>); AutoGen tries them in order, which gives fallback, lets you mix cloud and local models, and lets you route cheaper models first for cost. A local-only setup keeps data private and avoids API cost, at the price of hardware and usually weaker models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0019","card":"q-autogen-005-minimal-chatbot","sec":"autogen-s005","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what does <code>human_input_mode=\"NEVER\"</code> mean in a <code>ConversableAgent</code> call?","a":"<p>The agent never asks a human for input, so replies come only from the LLM and the run is fully automated. Without it, a <code>ConversableAgent</code> defaults to <code>\"TERMINATE\"</code>, which can prompt a human at the end of the chat.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent = ConversableAgent(\n    name=&quot;chatbot&quot;,\n    llm_config=llm_config,\n    human_input_mode=&quot;NEVER&quot;\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"autogen-f0020","card":"q-autogen-005-minimal-chatbot","sec":"autogen-s005","q":"How do you get one reply from an AutoGen v0.2 agent without starting a multi-turn chat, and what input does it need?","a":"<p>Call <code>agent.generate_reply(messages=[...])</code>. <code>messages</code> is a list of OpenAI-style dicts such as <code>{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"}</code>. It returns the reply (usually a string) and does not require another agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0021","card":"q-autogen-005-minimal-chatbot","sec":"autogen-s005","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what does <code>llm_config</code> allow a <code>ConversableAgent</code> to do, and what does <code>llm_config=False</code> mean?","a":"<p>It gives the agent an LLM, so it can understand context, converse in natural language and convert between structured and unstructured text. An agent with <code>llm_config=False</code> has no LLM and can only run code, tools or human input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0022","card":"q-autogen-006-debugging-a-broken-setup","sec":"autogen-s006","q":"An AutoGen v0.2 script runs but the agent returns nothing or errors on the LLM call. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check in order: the <code>.env</code> file exists and <code>load_dotenv()</code> returns <code>True</code>; <code>os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")</code> is not <code>None</code>; the model name in <code>llm_config</code> is valid for your account; and <code>pyautogen</code> is installed in the active environment. A wrong model name or missing key are the most common causes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0023","card":"q-autogen-006-debugging-a-broken-setup","sec":"autogen-s006","q":"<code>load_dotenv()</code> returns <code>False</code>. What does it mean and what is the fix?","a":"<p>No <code>.env</code> file was found (wrong working directory or file missing). Create it next to where you run the script, or pass an explicit path with <code>load_dotenv(path)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0024","card":"q-autogen-006-debugging-a-broken-setup","sec":"autogen-s006","q":"What quick checks confirm that an agent was built with the config you think it has?","a":"<p>Print <code>load_dotenv()</code> (should be <code>True</code>), <code>os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")</code> (should not be <code>None</code>, but do not log the real key in shared logs), the <code>llm_config</code> dict, and <code>agent.name</code>. These separate environment problems from agent-construction problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0025","card":"q-autogen-007-3-agent-types-and-human-input-modes","sec":"autogen-s007","q":"How do <code>ConversableAgent</code>, <code>AssistantAgent</code> and <code>UserProxyAgent</code> differ in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p><code>ConversableAgent</code> is the flexible base class. <code>AssistantAgent</code> is an LLM agent with a default system message for coding and language tasks, normally with code execution turned off. <code>UserProxyAgent</code> stands in for the human: it takes human input and can execute code. Pick <code>AssistantAgent</code> for the thinker, <code>UserProxyAgent</code> for the executor or human, and <code>ConversableAgent</code> for custom mixes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0026","card":"q-autogen-007-3-agent-types-and-human-input-modes","sec":"autogen-s007","q":"What are the three <code>human_input_mode</code> values in AutoGen v0.2 and when does each ask the human?","a":"<p><code>\"NEVER\"</code>: never asks, fully automatic. <code>\"ALWAYS\"</code>: asks at every turn, so the human can approve or redirect. <code>\"TERMINATE\"</code>: asks only when the conversation is about to end (termination condition or auto-reply limit reached).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0027","card":"q-autogen-007-3-agent-types-and-human-input-modes","sec":"autogen-s007","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how should you choose <code>human_input_mode</code> for an agent that runs code or calls a web search?","a":"<p>Use <code>\"ALWAYS\"</code> for risky actions (running code, writes, paid or external APIs) so a human approves each step. Use <code>\"TERMINATE\"</code> when the human should only check the final outcome, and <code>\"NEVER\"</code> for safe, fully automated steps. Pick by blast radius and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0028","card":"q-autogen-007-3-agent-types-and-human-input-modes","sec":"autogen-s007","q":"What are the default <code>human_input_mode</code> values of <code>UserProxyAgent</code> and <code>AssistantAgent</code> in AutoGen v0.2, and what surprise can that cause?","a":"<p><code>UserProxyAgent</code> defaults to <code>\"ALWAYS\"</code> and <code>AssistantAgent</code> to <code>\"NEVER\"</code>. A script that uses a default <code>UserProxyAgent</code> will stop and wait for keyboard input at each turn, which looks like a hang in automated runs. Set <code>human_input_mode=\"NEVER\"</code> explicitly for headless jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0029","card":"q-autogen-007-3-agent-types-and-human-input-modes","sec":"autogen-s007","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why is <code>code_execution_config=False</code> usually set on an <code>AssistantAgent</code>?","a":"<p>The assistant's job is to write code and text, not run it. Keeping execution off the LLM-driven agent and on a separate executor agent gives a clear safety boundary, because code is run only where you control approval, timeout and working directory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0030","card":"q-autogen-008-4-conversation-termination","sec":"autogen-s008","q":"How does <code>is_termination_msg</code> work on an AutoGen v0.2 agent?","a":"<p>You pass a function that receives the incoming message dict and returns <code>True</code> when the chat should stop. The check runs on received messages, so the agent stops replying once a message satisfies it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0031","card":"q-autogen-008-4-conversation-termination","sec":"autogen-s008","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, write an <code>is_termination_msg</code> check that stops when a message contains the word \"exit\", case-insensitive, and say why <code>msg.get(\"content\", \"\")</code> is used.","a":"<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def is_done(msg):\n    return &quot;exit&quot; in msg.get(&quot;content&quot;, &quot;&quot;).lower()</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>get(\"content\", \"\")</code> is used because some messages (such as pure tool calls) have no text content or <code>None</code>; the default avoids a <code>KeyError</code>. If <code>content</code> can be <code>None</code>, use <code>(msg.get(\"content\") or \"\")</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def is_done(msg):\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"autogen-f0032","card":"q-autogen-008-4-conversation-termination","sec":"autogen-s008","q":"What loop guards exist besides <code>is_termination_msg</code> in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p><code>max_turns</code> on <code>initiate_chat</code> or in a chat dict limits the turns in a two-agent chat. <code>max_consecutive_auto_reply</code> limits automatic replies per agent. <code>max_round</code> on <code>GroupChat</code> limits speaker selections. Code executors have a <code>timeout</code>. Use several, because a keyword-based termination can fail if the LLM never says the keyword.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0033","card":"q-autogen-008-4-conversation-termination","sec":"autogen-s008","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, two agents keep thanking each other forever and the chat never ends. What do you fix?","a":"<p>Add a termination condition (a keyword in the system message plus <code>is_termination_msg</code>), and set <code>max_turns</code> or <code>max_consecutive_auto_reply</code> as a hard cap. Keyword checks alone are fragile because the model may not follow the instruction, so keep the hard cap too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0034","card":"q-autogen-009-5-sequential-chats-research-assistant","sec":"autogen-s009","q":"What is a sequential chat in AutoGen v0.2 and when do you use it?","a":"<p>A list of two-agent chats that run one after another, started with <code>initiate_chats</code>, where the summary of each chat is passed on as context to the next. Use it for linear workflows in which each step needs the result of the previous one, such as idea, then draft, then review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0035","card":"q-autogen-009-5-sequential-chats-research-assistant","sec":"autogen-s009","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what three phases do you follow to build a sequential-chat workflow?","a":"<p>Create the agents (roles and system messages), define the tasks as a list of chat dicts, and call <code>initiate_chats</code> with that list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0036","card":"q-autogen-009-5-sequential-chats-research-assistant","sec":"autogen-s009","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 sequential chats (<code>initiate_chats</code>), why do prompts and system messages need to be specific?","a":"<p>Each step's output feeds the next, so vague instructions give vague summaries that degrade every later step. \"Generate 3-5 campaign ideas for product X with storylines\" works better than \"give me ideas\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0037","card":"q-autogen-010-agents","sec":"autogen-s010","q":"In an AutoGen v0.2 multi-agent setup, how should you write the <code>system_message</code> of a role agent?","a":"<p>State the role, list its responsibilities as short bullets, and say what output is expected. A focused role (for example \"Script Reviewer: check coherence, engagement, alignment with the topic\") with no overlap with other agents gives better, more predictable replies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0038","card":"q-autogen-011-tasks-the-chat-queue","sec":"autogen-s011","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>initiate_chats</code>, what do <code>sender</code>, <code>recipient</code> and <code>message</code> mean in each chat dict?","a":"<p><code>sender</code> is the agent that starts the chat, <code>recipient</code> is the agent that answers, and <code>message</code> is the opening instruction (a string, or a function when used in nested chats).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0039","card":"q-autogen-011-tasks-the-chat-queue","sec":"autogen-s011","q":"What are the two common values of <code>summary_method</code> in AutoGen v0.2, and how do they differ?","a":"<p><code>\"last_msg\"</code> uses the last message of the chat as the summary: fast and cheap, but may miss context. <code>\"reflection_with_llm\"</code> asks an LLM to write a summary (optionally guided by <code>summary_args={\"summary_prompt\": ...}</code>): smarter and more complete, but costs an extra LLM call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0040","card":"q-autogen-011-tasks-the-chat-queue","sec":"autogen-s011","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what does <code>clear_history</code> control in a chat dict used with <code>initiate_chats</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>True</code> the chat starts with no earlier messages, which suits an independent task. With <code>False</code> the chat keeps the context from previous chats, which suits a task that builds on them. Setting it wrongly either leaks irrelevant context or loses needed context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0041","card":"q-autogen-011-tasks-the-chat-queue","sec":"autogen-s011","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what does <code>max_turns</code> do in a chat dict passed to <code>initiate_chats</code>, and how do you choose its value?","a":"<p>It caps the number of conversation turns in that chat. <code>1</code> gives a single response; <code>2</code> or more allows back-and-forth refinement. A higher value improves iteration but costs more tokens, so use the smallest value that does the job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0042","card":"q-autogen-011-tasks-the-chat-queue","sec":"autogen-s011","q":"How does information flow from one chat to the next in AutoGen v0.2 <code>initiate_chats</code>?","a":"<p>Each chat produces a summary (per <code>summary_method</code>). That summary is carried forward as context for the following chat. So the quality of the summary directly decides what the next agent knows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0043","card":"q-autogen-011-tasks-the-chat-queue","sec":"autogen-s011","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>initiate_chats</code>, why might the last chat in the queue use the same agent as both <code>sender</code> and <code>recipient</code>?","a":"<p>It lets that agent produce a final decision or synthesis over the carried-over summaries without involving another agent. With a human-proxy agent it gives a final decision step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0044","card":"q-autogen-012-run-and-inspect","sec":"autogen-s012","q":"What does <code>initiate_chats</code> return in AutoGen v0.2 and what can you read from it?","a":"<p>A list of <code>ChatResult</code> objects, one per chat. Each holds fields such as <code>summary</code>, <code>cost</code>, and <code>chat_history</code>. Loop over them to print summaries or total the cost per step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0045","card":"q-autogen-012-run-and-inspect","sec":"autogen-s012","q":"How do you track the cost of a multi-chat run in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p>Read <code>chat_result.cost</code> for each result (token cost summary). Summing these per step shows which agent or step is expensive, so you can shorten prompts, cut <code>max_turns</code>, or use a cheaper model there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0046","card":"q-autogen-012-run-and-inspect","sec":"autogen-s012","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how can you add a follow-up chat to an <code>initiate_chats</code> workflow depending on an earlier result?","a":"<p>Run the queue, inspect an earlier <code>chat_result.summary</code> in code, and append or start another chat dict if a condition is met (for example the summary asks for revision). Because the chat list is ordinary Python data, you can build it dynamically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0047","card":"q-autogen-013-6-nested-chats-reflection-pattern","sec":"autogen-s013","q":"What is a nested chat in AutoGen v0.2 and which problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A conversation run inside another conversation: before an agent replies, it holds inner chats with other agents and then answers with their consolidated result. It models multi-step review, quality control and hierarchical decisions without exposing the inner steps to the outer conversation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0048","card":"q-autogen-013-6-nested-chats-reflection-pattern","sec":"autogen-s013","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how does the reflection pattern work with a writer agent and a reviewer agent that uses nested chats?","a":"<p>The reviewer sends the task to the writer. When the writer replies, the reviewer runs inner chats with specialist reviewers (for example SEO, grammar, ethics, then a meta reviewer), then sends the consolidated feedback back to the writer, who revises. The inner chats act like the reviewer's inner monologue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0049","card":"q-autogen-014-agents","sec":"autogen-s014","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what is the purpose of <code>is_termination_msg=lambda msg: \"TERMINATE\" in msg.get(\"content\", \"\")</code> on an agent?","a":"<p>It ends the chat when a message contains the keyword \"TERMINATE\". Without a stop condition or <code>max_turns</code>, the two agents may keep replying to each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0050","card":"q-autogen-015-specialized-reviewers","sec":"autogen-s015","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 nested chats, why split review into specialist reviewer agents (SEO, grammar, ethics) plus a meta reviewer, instead of one reviewer prompt?","a":"<p>Each specialist has a narrow prompt, so it checks its area more thoroughly and consistently. A meta reviewer then merges feedback into priorities and a verdict. The trade-off is one LLM call per reviewer, so cost grows with the number of specialists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0051","card":"q-autogen-015-specialized-reviewers","sec":"autogen-s015","q":"Besides content writing, where else does the AutoGen v0.2 nested-chat reviewer pattern fit?","a":"<p>Any task with several review dimensions: code review (security, performance, style, then a lead reviewer), paper review (methodology, statistics, ethics, then an editor), and compliance checks of content.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0052","card":"q-autogen-016-reflection-message-function","sec":"autogen-s016","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 nested chats, the <code>message</code> of a chat entry can be a function with signature <code>(recipient, messages, sender, config)</code>. Why use a function there?","a":"<p>It builds the message when the chat runs, so it can include the latest content automatically, for example <code>f\"Review the following content:\\n\\n{messages[-1]['content']}\"</code>. A fixed string cannot contain a draft that does not exist at configuration time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0053","card":"q-autogen-016-reflection-message-function","sec":"autogen-s016","q":"In an AutoGen v0.2 nested-chat message function <code>(recipient, messages, sender, config)</code>, what is <code>messages[-1]['content']</code>?","a":"<p><code>messages</code> is the history of the triggering conversation, so <code>messages[-1]</code> is the latest message (for example the writer's draft) and <code>['content']</code> is its text. Passing it avoids hard-coding text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0054","card":"q-autogen-017-review-chat-queue-and-registration","sec":"autogen-s017","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>register_nested_chats</code>, what do <code>chat_queue</code> and <code>trigger</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>chat_queue</code> is the list of nested chat dicts (recipient, message, summary settings, <code>max_turns</code>), run in sequence. <code>trigger</code> selects which senders activate them: here, whenever <code>writer</code> sends a message to <code>reviewer</code>, the inner chats run before the reviewer replies.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>reviewer.register_nested_chats(\n    chat_queue=review_chats,\n    trigger=writer\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"autogen-f0055","card":"q-autogen-017-review-chat-queue-and-registration","sec":"autogen-s017","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, a reviewer agent has nested chats registered with <code>register_nested_chats</code> and then chats with a writer agent. What is the execution order?","a":"<p>The reviewer sends the task to the writer, the writer drafts, the nested chats run in order (specialists, then meta reviewer), the reviewer sends the consolidated feedback to the writer, and the writer improves its draft.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0056","card":"q-autogen-017-review-chat-queue-and-registration","sec":"autogen-s017","q":"What forms can <code>trigger</code> take in AutoGen v0.2 <code>register_nested_chats</code>?","a":"<p>It can be an agent, an agent class, a name string, a callable that takes the sender and returns a boolean, or a list of these. A callable lets you trigger inner chats only when a condition holds (for example a message mentions a review).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0057","card":"q-autogen-018-review-chat-queue-and-registration","sec":"autogen-s017","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 nested chats, how do you choose <code>max_turns</code> for a review chat in the chat queue?","a":"<p>Use <code>1</code> for one focused specialist review (ask, answer, done). Use around 3 if the reviewer and specialist need a back-and-forth. Each additional turn adds cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0058","card":"q-autogen-018-review-chat-queue-and-registration","sec":"autogen-s017","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 nested chats, why should the final entry in a review chat queue be a meta reviewer, and what does it receive?","a":"<p>Earlier chat summaries are carried forward as context, so a final aggregator can merge the specialists' feedback into one assessment and verdict. The reply of a nested-chat queue is the summary of the last chat only (<code>res[-1].summary</code>). Without an aggregator at the end, the reply is just the last specialist's output and the other reviews are dropped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0059","card":"q-autogen-018-review-chat-queue-and-registration","sec":"autogen-s017","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, a reviewer <code>ConversableAgent</code> has nested chats registered and a writer agent drafts content. What does <code>reviewer.initiate_chat(recipient=writer, message=task, summary_method=\"last_msg\")</code> return, and what do you do with it?","a":"<p>It runs the whole conversation (with nested chats) and returns a <code>ChatResult</code>. <code>result.summary</code> holds the summary; with <code>\"last_msg\"</code> it is the last message, here the writer's final revised content.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0060","card":"q-autogen-019-7-tool-use-and-web-search","sec":"autogen-s018","q":"What is tool use for an LLM agent and why is it needed?","a":"<p>A tool is a function the LLM can ask to call. LLMs alone cannot compute exactly, fetch current data or touch external systems; tools add calculation, web search, database access and actions. The LLM picks the tool and arguments; your code runs it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0061","card":"q-autogen-019-7-tool-use-and-web-search","sec":"autogen-s018","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why are tools registered twice, once for the LLM and once for execution?","a":"<p>The assistant (LLM) only proposes a call: it needs the tool's name, description and argument schema. The executor agent (often with no LLM) actually runs the Python function. Separating them means the proposer cannot execute anything by itself, and you can put approval or sandboxing on the executor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0062","card":"q-autogen-019-7-tool-use-and-web-search","sec":"autogen-s018","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, a registered tool is never called by the LLM. What do you check?","a":"<p>That the description is clear about when to use it, that it is registered with both the caller (for LLM) and the executor, that type hints and docstring are accurate, and that the system message mentions the tool. Also check the model supports function/tool calling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0063","card":"q-autogen-019-7-tool-use-and-web-search","sec":"autogen-s018","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what stops an agent from looping forever on tool calls, and how do you handle risky tools?","a":"<p>Add a termination condition and cap turns or rounds. For risky tools set <code>human_input_mode</code> according to risk (for example <code>\"ALWAYS\"</code> for web calls or writes), validate tool inputs, and rate-limit external APIs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0064","card":"q-autogen-020-calculator-tool","sec":"autogen-s019","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why use <code>Annotated</code> and <code>Literal</code> in the type hints of a function that you register as a tool?","a":"<p>AutoGen builds the JSON schema for the LLM from the function signature. <code>Annotated[type, \"text\"]</code> adds a description to each argument, so the LLM knows what to pass. <code>Literal[\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\"]</code> restricts a value to an allowed set, which makes invalid operators far less likely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0065","card":"q-autogen-020-calculator-tool","sec":"autogen-s019","q":"What is wrong with this tool that registers a division operation?","a":"<p><code>//</code> is floor division, so <code>7 / 2</code> returns <code>3</code>, which silently gives wrong results for a division tool. Also dividing by zero raises <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. Use true division with a float return type and handle zero. Also <code>operator: str</code> lets the LLM send any text; a <code>Literal</code> type restricts it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def calc(a: int, b: int, operator: str) -&gt; int:\n    if operator == &quot;/&quot;:\n        return a // b\n    raise ValueError(&quot;Invalid operator&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"autogen-f0066","card":"q-autogen-020-calculator-tool","sec":"autogen-s019","q":"When an LLM is asked to compute a multi-step expression with a calculator tool, how does the flow go?","a":"<p>The assistant breaks the expression into several tool calls, the proxy executes each and returns the result, and the assistant combines the results into the final answer. That is more reliable than the LLM doing the arithmetic itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0067","card":"q-autogen-021-registering-tools-two-methods","sec":"autogen-s020","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what are the two ways to register a tool, and which should you prefer?","a":"<p>Direct: <code>assistant.register_for_llm(name=..., description=...)(func)</code> and <code>user_proxy.register_for_execution(name=...)(func)</code>, two calls. Helper: <code>register_function(func, caller=assistant, executor=user_proxy, name=..., description=...)</code>, one call. Prefer <code>register_function</code> for normal use because it is shorter and avoids forgetting one side or mismatching names; use the direct form when you need custom control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0068","card":"q-autogen-021-registering-tools-two-methods","sec":"autogen-s020","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>register_function(func, caller=..., executor=..., name=..., description=...)</code>, what are <code>caller</code> and <code>executor</code>?","a":"<p><code>caller</code> is the agent whose LLM may suggest calling the function. <code>executor</code> is the agent that actually runs it. They are usually two different agents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0069","card":"q-autogen-021-registering-tools-two-methods","sec":"autogen-s020","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why must <code>name</code> match when using the <code>register_for_llm</code> and <code>register_for_execution</code> decorators separately?","a":"<p>The LLM returns a function name in its call. The executor looks that name up among its registered functions. If the names differ, the call is not found and the tool fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0070","card":"q-autogen-021-registering-tools-two-methods","sec":"autogen-s020","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how do you give one assistant agent several tools?","a":"<p>Call <code>register_function</code> once per tool, each with its own <code>name</code> and a distinct, clear <code>description</code>, using the same caller and executor. Clear descriptions let the LLM choose the right tool, and tool chaining (search then calculate) happens as sequential tool calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0071","card":"q-autogen-022-web-search-with-tavily","sec":"autogen-s021","q":"How do you add web search to an AutoGen v0.2 agent?","a":"<p>Write a function that wraps a search API client (for example Tavily's <code>TavilyClient(...).search(query=...)</code>), annotate its argument, and register it with <code>register_function</code> so the assistant can call it and a proxy agent executes it. Keep the API key in an environment variable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0072","card":"q-autogen-022-web-search-with-tavily","sec":"autogen-s021","q":"What is ReAct prompting and why does it help agents?","a":"<p>ReAct (Reasoning plus Acting) makes the LLM alternate Thought, Action, Observation until it can give a Final Answer. Reasoning before and after each tool call improves accuracy and makes the decisions visible. Cost: more tokens and calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0073","card":"q-autogen-022-web-search-with-tavily","sec":"autogen-s021","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why would you set <code>human_input_mode=\"ALWAYS\"</code> on the agent that executes a web search tool?","a":"<p>So a human approves each search before it runs, which controls cost, data sent out and prompt-injection risk from search results. Use <code>\"NEVER\"</code> only when searches are low-risk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0074","card":"q-autogen-022-web-search-with-tavily","sec":"autogen-s021","q":"Why is a search tool needed for a question such as the latest sports result?","a":"<p>An LLM only knows its training data, which has a cutoff date. A search tool supplies current information, and the assistant uses the results to answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0075","card":"q-autogen-023-8-coding-copilot-code-execution","sec":"autogen-s022","q":"How does the two-agent code-execution pattern work in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p>A writer agent (LLM, no execution) produces code in markdown blocks. An executor agent (no LLM) runs the code and returns output or errors. Errors go back to the writer, which fixes the code, repeating until it succeeds. The split keeps generation and execution separate so you can control what runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0076","card":"q-autogen-023-8-coding-copilot-code-execution","sec":"autogen-s022","q":"What are the safety rules for letting an agent run LLM-written code?","a":"<p>Review before running (<code>human_input_mode=\"ALWAYS\"</code> on the executor), set a <code>timeout</code>, use an isolated working directory, and prefer a container (<code>DockerCommandLineCodeExecutor</code>) over running directly on the host. LLM-written code is untrusted and could delete files or leak data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0077","card":"q-autogen-023-8-coding-copilot-code-execution","sec":"autogen-s022","q":"Local command-line executor versus Docker executor in AutoGen v0.2: how do they differ?","a":"<p><code>LocalCommandLineCodeExecutor</code> runs code directly on your machine with your permissions: simple, but unsafe for untrusted code. <code>DockerCommandLineCodeExecutor</code> runs it in a container: isolated, but needs Docker and image setup. Use Docker for anything not fully trusted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0078","card":"q-autogen-024-executor-and-agents","sec":"autogen-s023","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what do the <code>timeout</code> and <code>work_dir</code> parameters of <code>LocalCommandLineCodeExecutor</code> do?","a":"<p><code>timeout</code> is the maximum seconds a code run may take before it is stopped, which protects against infinite loops. <code>work_dir</code> is the folder where code files are saved and run, so outputs land there. Use a dedicated folder to isolate side effects.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>executor = LocalCommandLineCodeExecutor(\n    timeout=10,\n    work_dir=&quot;coding&quot;\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"autogen-f0079","card":"q-autogen-024-executor-and-agents","sec":"autogen-s023","q":"How do you connect an executor to an agent in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p>Pass <code>code_execution_config={\"executor\": executor}</code> when creating the agent, and set <code>llm_config=False</code> if the agent should only run code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0080","card":"q-autogen-024-executor-and-agents","sec":"autogen-s023","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why give the executor agent <code>llm_config=False</code> and a <code>code_execution_config</code>?","a":"<p>The executor only runs code and returns output or errors; it does not need to think. Turning the LLM off saves cost and makes it deterministic, so the execution point stays controlled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0081","card":"q-autogen-024-executor-and-agents","sec":"autogen-s023","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what is the role of <code>default_auto_reply</code> on a code executor agent?","a":"<p>It is the message the agent sends when it has nothing else to say, for example when the writer's message contains no code block. A text such as \"Continue. Please keep solving the task until done.\" nudges the writer to keep working or produce code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0082","card":"q-autogen-024-executor-and-agents","sec":"autogen-s023","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why must an LLM agent put code in a fenced markdown code block for the executor agent to run it?","a":"<p>The executor agent finds code to run by extracting fenced code blocks from messages. Code outside a fenced block is ignored.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0083","card":"q-autogen-025-running-a-task","sec":"autogen-s024","q":"What is the order of steps in a coding-copilot chat in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p>The executor sends the task to the writer, the writer replies with code in a markdown block, the executor asks the human for approval (if <code>ALWAYS</code>), runs the code in <code>work_dir</code>, and sends the output or error back. The writer then confirms success or debugs, until the task is done or a termination rule fires.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0084","card":"q-autogen-025-running-a-task","sec":"autogen-s024","q":"In an AutoGen v0.2 code-writing agent, why might the date ranges it writes for a \"year-to-date\" request be wrong, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The LLM does not know today's date. Insert it into the instruction (for example with <code>date.today()</code> in an f-string) so the code uses correct ranges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0085","card":"q-autogen-026-custom-functions-reduce-hallucination-re","sec":"autogen-s025","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why give a code executor your own helper functions instead of letting the LLM write all the code?","a":"<p>Tested, reusable functions reduce hallucinated or inconsistent code: the LLM calls known functions (for example to fetch and plot data) instead of rewriting the logic each time. It also gives consistent behaviour and shorter outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0086","card":"q-autogen-026-custom-functions-reduce-hallucination-re","sec":"autogen-s025","q":"How do you make helper functions available to code run by <code>LocalCommandLineCodeExecutor</code> in AutoGen v0.2, and how does the writer learn about them?","a":"<p>Pass them in <code>functions=[...]</code> when you create the executor. Then append <code>format_functions_for_prompt(executor.functions)</code> to the writer's system message. That helper extracts signatures and docstrings so the LLM knows what exists and how to call it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0087","card":"q-autogen-026-custom-functions-reduce-hallucination-re","sec":"autogen-s025","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, after adding custom functions to a <code>LocalCommandLineCodeExecutor</code>, why must the executor agent be linked to the new executor?","a":"<p>The agent keeps the executor object you gave it. If you create a second executor with <code>functions=[...]</code> but the agent still points to the old one, the code the writer produces will not find the functions. Rebuild the agent (or reassign) with <code>code_execution_config={\"executor\": new_executor}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0088","card":"q-autogen-026-custom-functions-reduce-hallucination-re","sec":"autogen-s025","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, which failure causes would you check when code execution by an executor agent fails?","a":"<p>Timeout too low, <code>work_dir</code> missing, required libraries not installed in the environment that runs the code, and bad code from the writer (the error message goes back to the writer for a fix).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0089","card":"q-autogen-027-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"What problem do teachable agents solve?","a":"<p>A standard LLM chat forgets everything when the session ends, in-context learning is not persistent, context windows fill up and users repeat their instructions. A teachable agent stores what it learns so it carries over to later sessions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0090","card":"q-autogen-027-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"How does AutoGen's <code>Teachability</code> capability work in v0.2?","a":"<p>When a user teaches something (fact, preference or skill), the agent writes a memo, embeds it and stores it in a vector database (ChromaDB). Later, each new query is embedded, a similarity search retrieves relevant memos, and these are added to the prompt so the reply uses them. Memory therefore survives restarts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0091","card":"q-autogen-027-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"How do you add memory to an agent in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p>Install <code>pyautogen[teachable]</code>, create <code>Teachability(reset_db=False, path_to_db_dir=...)</code> and call <code>teachability.add_to_agent(agent)</code>. It adds memory to an existing <code>ConversableAgent</code>; you do not need a special agent class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0092","card":"q-autogen-027-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"What do <code>reset_db</code> and <code>path_to_db_dir</code> do in <code>Teachability</code>?","a":"<p><code>reset_db=True</code> clears the stored memory at start; <code>False</code> loads what is on disk. <code>path_to_db_dir</code> is where the database lives. Setting <code>reset_db=True</code> by mistake wipes learned memory on every restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0093","card":"q-autogen-027-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"What kinds of knowledge can a teachable agent learn, and how do you teach each?","a":"<p>Facts (\"My birthday is June 15\"), preferences (\"I prefer bullet-point summaries\") and skills or procedures (\"When solving math problems follow these steps\"). Use explicit phrasing such as \"I always prefer...\" or \"Remember that...\", teach one concept at a time and verify with a follow-up question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0094","card":"q-autogen-028-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"Teachable agent versus a standard agent: what are the differences?","a":"<p>Standard: memory lasts one session, no personalisation, instructions repeated each time, context limited to the window. Teachable: persistent memory, personalisation grows with use, one-time teaching, and context extended by retrieval from the database. The cost is extra embedding and retrieval work and the need to manage stored data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0095","card":"q-autogen-028-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how do you keep <code>Teachability</code> memory separate for each user in a teachable-agent service?","a":"<p>Give each user their own <code>path_to_db_dir</code> (for example <code>./db/&lt;user_id&gt;</code>) and build the agent with its own <code>Teachability</code>. One shared database would mix users' private memos.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0096","card":"q-autogen-028-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"A teachable agent forgets what you taught it after a restart. What do you check?","a":"<p>That <code>reset_db=False</code>, that <code>path_to_db_dir</code> is the same path as before, and that the run prints that it is loading memory from disk. If the database is corrupt, delete the folder and restart with <code>reset_db=True</code>, which loses the old memos.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0097","card":"q-autogen-028-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"How does the retrieval step in a teachable agent differ from putting all past chats in the prompt?","a":"<p>Only memos similar to the current query are fetched by vector search, so the prompt stays short and relevant. Sending the full history would hit the context-window limit and cost more tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0098","card":"q-autogen-028-9-teachable-agents-long-term-memory","sec":"autogen-s026","q":"What privacy and quality risks come with persistent agent memory?","a":"<p>Stored personal data must be protected, deletable and kept per user. Wrong or outdated memos can be retrieved later and mislead the agent, so provide a way to inspect, correct or clear the memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0099","card":"q-autogen-029-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"What is a group chat in AutoGen v0.2 and what does the <code>GroupChatManager</code> do?","a":"<p>A group chat lets several specialised agents work on a task in one shared conversation. The <code>GroupChatManager</code> selects the next speaker (using an LLM by default) and broadcasts each message to all the agents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0100","card":"q-autogen-029-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"Why does <code>GroupChatManager</code> need an <code>llm_config</code>, and what happens without one?","a":"<p>Its LLM chooses which agent speaks next. Without an LLM config the default automatic speaker selection cannot work, unless you choose a non-LLM selection method such as round robin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0101","card":"q-autogen-029-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"What do <code>agents</code>, <code>messages</code> and <code>max_round</code> mean in AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>?","a":"<p><code>agents</code> is the list of participants, <code>messages</code> is the shared history (usually an empty list at the start), and <code>max_round</code> is the maximum number of speaker turns. <code>max_round</code> prevents endless conversations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0102","card":"q-autogen-029-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"How do you start a group chat in AutoGen v0.2?","a":"<p>Call <code>admin.initiate_chat(chat_manager, message=task)</code> where <code>admin</code> is a participant (often a <code>UserProxyAgent</code>) and <code>chat_manager</code> is the <code>GroupChatManager</code>. The manager then runs the rounds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0103","card":"q-autogen-029-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, why set <code>use_docker</code> in an agent's <code>code_execution_config</code>, and what is the risk of <code>False</code>?","a":"<p><code>use_docker=False</code> runs code directly on the host instead of inside a container. It avoids needing Docker, but LLM-written code then runs with your permissions, so use it only in trusted or disposable environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0104","card":"q-autogen-030-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, what does <code>allowed_or_disallowed_speaker_transitions</code> do in <code>GroupChat</code>?","a":"<p>It is a dictionary mapping each agent to the agents that may speak right after it. The manager then chooses the next speaker only from allowed agents. It turns a free-for-all into a controlled flow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0105","card":"q-autogen-030-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>, what does <code>speaker_transitions_type</code> change?","a":"<p>With <code>\"allowed\"</code>, the dictionary lists the only permitted transitions (strict pathways). With <code>\"disallowed\"</code>, it lists forbidden transitions and everything else is allowed (flexibility with guardrails).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0106","card":"q-autogen-030-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>, how would you model a hierarchy such as a CEO, two managers and their workers with speaker transitions?","a":"<p>Make a transitions dict in which the CEO can go to managers, each manager can go to its own workers and back to the CEO, and each worker can only go back to its manager. Use <code>\"allowed\"</code>. Workers cannot talk across teams or to the CEO.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0107","card":"q-autogen-030-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>, why use speaker transition policies, and what is the downside?","a":"<p>They give structured communication, logical information flow, enforced review checkpoints and fewer wasted turns (lower token cost). The downside is that over-tight rules can leave an agent with nowhere valid to hand off, or cause loops between a few agents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0108","card":"q-autogen-030-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"In an AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>, the wrong agent speaks or the chat loops forever. What do you check?","a":"<p>For loops: set <code>max_round</code>, look for circular transitions and add a termination condition. For wrong speakers: check the transitions dictionary, make sure the manager has an LLM config, and make sure the agent objects used as dictionary keys are the same ones in <code>agents</code>. Also sharpen each agent's system message so roles do not overlap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0109","card":"q-autogen-030-10-group-chat-and-speaker-policies","sec":"autogen-s027","q":"What are best practices for designing agents in an AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>?","a":"<p>Give each agent one focused role with no overlap, test each agent alone first, add a termination condition, and keep <code>max_round</code> sensible. Add intermediate agents or revise transitions if collaboration is poor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0110","card":"q-autogen-031-speaker-selection-policy","sec":"autogen-s028","q":"What speaker selection methods does AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code> offer through <code>speaker_selection_method</code>?","a":"<p><code>\"auto\"</code> (default, the LLM chooses), <code>\"round_robin\"</code> (fixed rotation), <code>\"random\"</code>, <code>\"manual\"</code> (a human chooses), or a custom function that returns the next speaker. Use round robin for fixed pipelines, auto for flexible tasks, and a custom function for exact control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0111","card":"q-autogen-031-speaker-selection-policy","sec":"autogen-s028","q":"In AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code>, which would you use for strict control of who speaks next: an <code>\"allowed\"</code> transitions dict or <code>speaker_selection_method=\"round_robin\"</code>?","a":"<p>A transitions dict restricts choices but the LLM still picks among the allowed agents. Round robin removes the LLM choice and always rotates in order. Use transitions for guided flexibility and round robin when the order must be fixed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0112","card":"q-autogen-032-11-pattern-comparison-and-troubleshootin","sec":"autogen-s029","q":"How do sequential chats, nested chats and group chat compare, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Sequential (<code>initiate_chats</code>): fixed order, summaries carried forward, simplest setup, best for linear workflows. Nested (<code>register_nested_chats</code>): inner consultations triggered by an agent's message, medium setup, best for review and quality loops. Group chat (<code>GroupChat</code> plus <code>GroupChatManager</code>): LLM picks the speaker, most flexible and the most setup and cost, best for complex, unpredictable tasks (restrict it with speaker policies).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0113","card":"q-autogen-032-11-pattern-comparison-and-troubleshootin","sec":"autogen-s029","q":"What are common failure modes of AutoGen systems and their first checks?","a":"<p>Infinite loops: add termination, <code>max_turns</code>, <code>max_round</code> and timeouts. Tool never called: description and registration. Wrong speaker in group chat: policy dictionary and manager LLM. API key errors: <code>.env</code> and <code>load_dotenv()</code>. Memory not loading: <code>reset_db</code> and path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0114","card":"q-autogen-032-11-pattern-comparison-and-troubleshootin","sec":"autogen-s029","q":"An AutoGen v0.2 <code>GroupChat</code> costs too much and wanders off topic. How could you reduce cost and improve focus?","a":"<p>Add speaker transition rules, lower <code>max_round</code>, shorten system messages, use a cheaper model for simple agents, or replace parts with a fixed sequential chain when the order is known.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0115","card":"q-autogen-033-12-production-testing-projects-and-caree","sec":"autogen-s030","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how do you reduce token cost in an application?","a":"<p>Use concise system messages, cap output with <code>max_tokens</code> in <code>llm_config</code>, cache responses, reuse agent instances, limit turns and rounds, and track spending with <code>chat_result.cost</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0116","card":"q-autogen-033-12-production-testing-projects-and-caree","sec":"autogen-s030","q":"In AutoGen v0.2, how would you unit test an agent without calling a real LLM every time?","a":"<p>Test agent construction and wiring directly, and call <code>generate_reply</code> against a test config or a mocked LLM client, asserting that you get a non-empty reply and the right shape. LLM output is not deterministic, so assert on structure and key facts, not exact text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0117","card":"q-autogen-033-12-production-testing-projects-and-caree","sec":"autogen-s030","q":"What do you test in integration tests for multi-agent systems?","a":"<p>Multi-agent conversations end properly, tools get executed, errors are handled, and the output format is correct. Use low <code>max_turns</code>, cheap models and fixed prompts to keep runs affordable and stable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0118","card":"q-autogen-033-12-production-testing-projects-and-caree","sec":"autogen-s030","q":"What belongs on a production checklist for an AutoGen service?","a":"<p>Secrets in environment variables or a secret manager, logging and monitoring, rate limits and quotas, cost monitoring, input validation and sanitisation, human oversight for critical decisions, bias audits and disclosure that AI is in use. Code execution should be sandboxed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"autogen-f0119","card":"q-autogen-033-12-production-testing-projects-and-caree","sec":"autogen-s030","q":"In an AutoGen v0.2 <code>llm_config</code>, which settings do <code>max_tokens</code> and <code>temperature</code> control?","a":"<p><code>max_tokens</code> caps the length of the reply, which limits cost. <code>temperature</code> controls randomness: lower is more deterministic, higher is more varied. Use low values for tools and code, higher for creative writing.</p>","hand":true}];
